# C# Async Hygiene

## Banned in production code

- **Blocking on a task:** `.Result`, `.Wait()`, `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()`, including `Task.Run(() => SomeAsync()).Wait()`. Await it and make every caller up the chain `async`; do not stop the propagation with `.Result` to avoid changing a signature. Test classes whose name ends in `Tests`, `Fixtures` or `TestHelper` may block only when the test framework offers no async alternative.
- **`async void`:** return `Task`. The one exception is an event handler whose delegate signature requires `void`; suppress the warning inline with a comment saying why.
- **`Task.Run()` inside a controller action or service method:** it does not make I/O async. Call the async data-access method. When the only available method is synchronous and cannot be changed, call it synchronously with no `Task.Run` wrapper and document why no async version exists.

## Banned — overlapping EF operations on the same scoped DbContext

`AddDbContext` registers a context as scoped, one instance per request, and no DbContext is thread-safe. Every repository and service in the same request that takes the context shares that instance.

Do not start a second EF operation before the previous one completes:

```csharp
// banned: WhenAll over repositories that share the scoped context
await Task.WhenAll(itemsTask, totalsTask, countsTask);

// banned: both started before either is awaited
var a = _widgetRepository.GetItemsAsync(...);
var b = _widgetRepository.GetTotalsAsync(...);
await a;
await b;

// correct
var items = await _widgetRepository.GetItemsAsync(...);
var totals = await _widgetRepository.GetTotalsAsync(...);
```

`Task.WhenAll` is still fine for HTTP calls, email and other I/O that does not touch the scoped context. The ban is overlapping work that hits the same scoped context, including two different repositories in one service method.

When work must run in parallel, as in a batch job, give each branch its own context: the codebase's per-call repository if it has one (skill `dotnet-data-access`, Choosing a repository type), an `IDbContextFactory<TContext>` registered with `AddDbContextFactory`, or a new DI scope per branch.

Symptom: `InvalidOperationException: A second operation was started on this context instance before a previous operation completed.`

## ConfigureAwait

ASP.NET Core and the .NET generic host have no `SynchronizationContext`, so in code only they host, `ConfigureAwait(false)` changes nothing. Follow the codebase's convention there, which is usually to omit it.

- A class-library `.csproj` is not by itself a reason to add it. Check which processes host the assembly.
- In an assembly published as a NuGet package, or hosted by a UI or classic ASP.NET app, code that doesn't need the caller's context uses it on every await. Code that touches UI controls or `HttpContext.Current` after an await must not.
- When the convention is to omit it and a file you edit still has leftovers, strip them from the awaits you touch. Do not run a solution-wide cleanup unless asked.
